out. So maybe B4 is not useful.\\n\\nAnother angle: the user has two B7s. If they discard one, they still have one left. If they keep both, they might be able to form a Pung if they draw another. But there are only two B7s left in the wall. So if they discard one, they still have one, which could be part of a Pung if they draw another. So playing B7 is a good option.\\n\\nTherefore, the best move is to play B7. This preserves the B1-B2-B3 Chow possibility and the B4 pair. It also removes a tile that's part of a pair, which could help in forming the necessary melds and pair later.\\n\", \"output\": \"Evaluation: Playing B7 is the optimal move as it preserves the potential to form a Chow (B1-B2-B3) and a Pung (B4) while removing a duplicate tile that could be claimed by others.  \\nReason: Discarding B7 maintains flexibility for forming key melds in the Dots suit and avoids wasting a tile that may be less critical for the user's hand structure.  \\nAnswer: Play B7\"}"}
