w is better. But to do that, I need to have all three in my hand. Since I have two T1s, one T2, one T3, I can form the chow if I keep them. So playing T1 would reduce my chances of forming that chow. Therefore, maybe it's better to keep all three and not play T1. But the valid actions require me to play one of the listed tiles. Wait, the valid actions are the tiles I can play now. The options include T1, T2, T3. So if I play T1, I have another T1 left. Then I can still form the chow with T1, T2, T3. So playing T1 is safe.\n\nAlternatively, if I play T3, someone might take it for a chow, but I still have T1 and T2. But playing T3 could help others, which is not ideal. So the best move is to play T1, as it's part of the potential chow and I have another T1. That way, I can still form the chow if needed.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Playing T1 allows the formation of a potential chow (T1-T2-T3) while retaining another T1 in hand, maximizing flexibility. Discarding T1 is safe given the remaining supply and supports future meld development.\nReason: Forming the T1-T2-T3 chow would add a pure chow to the hand, improving scoring potential. Playing T1 now avoids blocking others and maintains the possibility to complete the chow with the remaining T1, T2, and T3.\nAnswer: Play T1"}
