ly, discarding T5 or T6 might help others form chows. \n\nIf I discard B9, I lose a terminal tile, but it's not part of any chow in the current hand. The other option is to discard a tile from the T1-2-3 sequence. For example, if I discard T2, I break the possibility of forming the T1-2-3 chow. But if I keep T1, T2, T3, I can form that chow. So it's better to keep those tiles. \n\nTherefore, the best option is to discard B9, as it's a terminal and not part of any chow in the current hand. That way, I preserve the possibility of forming the T1-2-3 chow and avoid giving others a useful tile.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The current hand has potential to form three chows (T1-2-3, T6-7-8, B1-2-3) and a pair. Discarding B9, a terminal tile not part of any chow, minimizes risk while preserving key tiles for forming additional melds.  \nReason: B9 is a terminal tile with low utility for chows and less likely to aid opponents. Keeping T1-2-3 intact maintains the possibility of completing the third chow, which is critical for achieving a \"Pure Straight\" or \"Mixed Straight\" pattern.  \nAnswer: Play B9"}
